{{Year article header|2025}}

The year saw an escalation of major ongoing armed conflicts,<ref>{{Cite web |last=McGurk |first=Brett H. |date=2025-07-29 |title=Analysis: The wars Trump promised to end are escalating. Why?|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/07/29/politics/trump-promises-gaza-ukraine-mcgurk-analysis |access-date=2025-08-11 |publisher=CNN |language=en}}</ref> including the [[Gaza war]], marked by [[Gaza Strip famine|famine]] and [[Gaza humanitarian crisis|a humanitarian crisis]] and the [[Russo-Ukrainian war (2022–present)|Russian invasion of Ukraine]], which prompted peace negotiations involving [[Vladimir Putin]] and [[Donald Trump]]; civil wars in [[Myanmar civil war (2021–present)|Myanmar]], [[Sudanese civil war (2023–present)|Sudan]] and [[Yemeni civil war (2014–present)|Yemen]] also continued throughout the year.

Internal crises in [[Armenian political crisis (2020–present)|Armenia]], [[Bangladesh post-resignation violence (2024–2026)|Bangladesh]], [[Bulgarian political crisis (2021–2026)|Bulgaria]], [[M23 campaign (2022–present)|the Democratic Republic of the Congo]], [[Ecuadorian security crisis|Ecuador]], [[2024–2025 French political crisis|France]], [[2024–2025 Georgian political crisis|Georgia]], [[German economic crisis (2022–present)|Germany]], [[Haitian crisis (2018–present)|Haiti]], [[Libyan crisis|Libya]], [[2022–2025 Pakistan political unrest|Pakistan]], [[Peruvian political crisis (2016–present)|Peru]], [[Constitutional crisis in Somalia|Somalia]], and [[2024 South Korean martial law crisis|South Korea]] persisted into the year, with the latter culminating in the [[Arrest of Yoon Suk Yeol|arrest]] and [[Impeachment of Yoon Suk Yeol|removal]] of President [[Yoon Suk Yeol]] from office. Furthermore, a [[2025–2026 China–Japan diplomatic crisis|diplomatic crisis between China and Japan]] broke out in November.

The year also witnessed a [[Gen Z protests|wave of protests]] predominantly led by [[Generation Z]], with some movements—such as [[2025 Nepalese Gen Z protests|those in Nepal]] and [[2025 Malagasy protests|Madagascar]]—resulting in the overthrow of targeted governments. Several brief conflicts arising from longstanding tensions emerged mid-year, including clashes between [[2025 India–Pakistan conflict|India and Pakistan]] in May, [[Twelve-Day War|Iran and Israel]] in June, and [[2025 Cambodia–Thailand border conflict|Cambodia and Thailand]] in July. In the latter case, a [[Thailand–Cambodia phone call leak|leaked phone call]] involving Thai prime minister [[Paetongtarn Shinawatra]] and Cambodian senate president [[Hun Sen]] [[2025 Thai political crisis|resulted in the removal]] of the former from office.

In economics and business, the [[Second inauguration of Donald Trump|return of Donald Trump]] to the [[President of the United States|U.S. presidency]] ushered in [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|a series of tariffs imposed by the United States on the rest of the world]], significantly disrupting global trade and reinvigorating the [[China–United States trade war]]. The technology sector was also affected by the release of the [[DeepSeek (chatbot)|DeepSeek]] chatbot, a Chinese [[large language model]] that competed with [[ChatGPT]].

Aviation and aerospace experienced several accidents during the year, including [[2025 Potomac River mid-air collision|a collision between two aircraft half a mile south of]] [[Ronald Reagan Washington National Airport|Washington National Airport]] in January, and the crash of [[Air India Flight 171]], a [[Boeing 787 Dreamliner|Boeing 787]] bound for [[Gatwick Airport|Gatwick]], in [[Ahmedabad]], India, in June. The year also saw notable advances in space exploration, including the [[Fram2|first crewed polar-orbit spaceflight]] and the [[Blue Ghost Mission 1|first fully successful lunar landing by a private company]].

